\name{readers_read_records}
\alias{readers_read_records}
\title{Read any file mzLib recognises, into that format's own fields}
\usage{
readers_read_records(path, limit = NULL, offset = 0, out = NULL, timeout = NULL)
}
\arguments{
\item{path}{Path to any file mzLib recognises. A Bruker \code{.d} directory is also accepted.}

\item{limit}{Maximum records to return. \code{NULL}, the default, returns all of them.}

\item{offset}{Records to skip. A window, not a cursor - see \code{\link{readers_read_results}}.}

\item{out}{Write a \strong{tab-separated} table here and return only a summary. Read it with
  \code{read.delim()}, not \code{read.csv()}.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
Read any file mzLib recognises into a table of that format's own record fields, naming every field that could not become a column.

The exhaustive verb: if \code{\link{readers_identify}} succeeds on a path, this reads it. All 31 file
types, including the 14 that belong to no cross-format view at all - TopPIC, Crux, MSFragger's
peptide and protein tables, the FlashDeconv formats, SDRF - which no other \code{readers_} function
can touch.
}
\details{
\strong{For SDRF, use \code{\link{sdrf_read}} instead.} This function joins each SDRF row's cells into one
semicolon-separated string, and SDRF's \code{NT=...;AC=...} grammar puts semicolons inside cells, so
the joined string cannot be split back apart.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_native_records}. \code{records} is a data.frame of this format's own fields, or
  \code{NULL} when \code{out} was given. \code{record_count} counts the records in the whole file,
  \code{returned_count} the records returned, starting \code{offset} records in. \code{column_names},
  \code{record_type}, \code{views}, \code{excluded_fields} and \code{failed_fields} describe the table.

}
\section{The columns are not uniform, by design}{


They are this format's own mzLib record fields, under mzLib's names in \code{snake_case}: a TopPIC
file gives 36 columns, a Crux file 23, an experiment annotation 5. Read \code{column_names}, and use
\code{\link{readers_read_results}}, \code{\link{readers_read_features}} or \code{\link{readers_read_matches}} when you need
columns that mean the same thing across formats.

Because the names are mzLib's own, they are \strong{cross-referenceable against the mzLib source}: a
column called \code{e_value} is \code{ToppicPrsm$EValue}, and \code{record_type} names the class to look in.

}
\section{Nothing is silently dropped}{


\code{excluded_fields} is a data.frame of the fields that could \strong{not} become columns, each with the
reason - a nested object or a dictionary has no faithful column shape, and inventing one would
mean publishing a schema mzLib does not have. A column that simply vanished would be
indistinguishable from a field the format does not have.

\code{failed_fields} names the fields that \strong{raised} while being read, with the exception type.
Several mzLib properties are computed and assume a UniProt-style FASTA header - Crux's and
MsPathFinderT's \code{accession} are both \code{protein_id} split on a pipe - so on other databases they
throw. Those cells arrive \code{NA} rather than taking the whole read down, but a failure must not
look like missing data.

Note that mzLib's documented \code{-1} "absent" sentinel is \strong{not} mapped to \code{NA} here, unlike in
\code{\link{readers_read_results}}. In a format's own columns \code{-1} is frequently a real measurement - a
mass difference, a delta, TopPIC's \code{feature_score} - and nulling those would destroy data.

}
